      "modify_from": "For tool_push: grasp near one end of the bar, leaving most of its length projecting from the hand. Choose the end nearer the robot, with the grasp center about 0.02 inward from that end. Initially approach this grasp center at z=0.20 with the gripper open and yaw along the bar's long axis modulo pi. When open above that grasp point, descend to z=0.01 and close. When closed low holding the bar at its original location, lift to z=0.20 at the same horizontal grasp point.",
      "modify_to": "For tool_push: grasp near one end of the bar, leaving most of its length projecting from the hand. Identify the physical endpoint nearer the robot in world coordinates, not by image left or right. Define the horizontal grasp point G approximately 0.05 inward from that endpoint along the bar's longitudinal centerline. Localize the bar using calibrated views, accounting for the elevation of its visible surface rather than projecting that surface directly onto the table. Use the retained initial scene and current calibrated wrist view to refine localization consistently without changing this definition of G. Initially approach G at z=0.20 with the gripper open and yaw along the bar's long axis modulo pi. Once the open hand is above G within the stated arrival tolerance, descend to G at z=0.01 and close. Do not substitute the measured, potentially undershooting TCP position for G or change the inward offset when the endpoint is observed again. When closed low holding the bar at its original location, lift to G at z=0.20. Use the same geometrically defined G and preserve the bar-aligned grasp yaw throughout approach, grasp descent, and lift."
    }
  ],
  "refused": "No additional release rule or memory field is committed. The existing release instruction already distinguishes arrival tolerance from release position: retain the nominal endpoint when it differs from the current pose by at least 0.002 m, and use the small upward displacement only otherwise. That instruction remains unchanged."
}
